# 1.1 Requirements and architecture

Read this reference when starting a new hardware design or changing the main function, power structure, external interface, or core part strategy of an existing project.

This stage decides what to design and how its parts cooperate. Stable results enter section 1.x of `CURRENT_HANDOFF.md`.

## Define the objective and boundaries

Organize what the product actually needs:

- use case and primary functions;
- inputs, outputs, and external interfaces;
- power sources, operating ranges, and main loads;
- dimensions, enclosure, environment, and mounting;
- cost, quantity, inventory, and purchasing constraints;
- functions the user explicitly excludes;
- what completion means for the current stage.

Ask only questions that change architecture, major parts, or acceptance. Ordinary gaps that can be checked safely on the prototype may become explicit tests.

Write the result under "Product objective and boundaries" in the manuscript.

## Build the functional architecture

Split the product into functional blocks that can be explained and tested independently.

For each block, describe:

- its purpose;
- inputs and outputs;
- power source;
- interfaces to other blocks;
- startup, shutdown, and default state;
- how its main faults affect other blocks;
- the simplest way to test it.

Make blocks only detailed enough to support part and schematic design. Do not expand every component and pin yet.

Write the blocks and their relationships under "Functional architecture".

## Build the power tree

Describe each source, conversion stage, and major consumer. Record:

- normal, minimum, and maximum voltage;
- continuous and peak current;
- startup sequence;
- enable and shutdown conditions;
- reverse-polarity, surge, short-circuit, overcurrent, or undervoltage behavior;
- high-current and switching nodes that may affect PCB design.

Average power does not replace peak, startup, and fault conditions. Calculate enough to choose the architecture and main parts without creating false precision for details that are not yet known.

Write the power tree and major power conditions under "Power tree and interfaces".

## Define interfaces

For external interfaces and important boundaries between blocks, record:

- signal or power direction;
- protocol and voltage domain;
- connector or cable conditions;
- hot-plug, disconnect, and misconnection behavior;
- default state and recovery;
- firmware, mechanical, or enclosure conditions that must also hold.

Stabilize external behavior before optimizing the internal implementation. Update the architecture first when external behavior changes.

## Default and fault states

For paths that affect product function or safety, describe:

- power-up and reset state;
- state before the controller initializes;
- state when a control signal is open or unpowered;
- behavior with insufficient input, excessive load, or high temperature;
- recovery after protection trips;
- user-visible or measurable state.

When current sources cannot establish the behavior of a protection, isolation, or fault-shutdown block, enter CURIOUS_MODE only for that question.

## Cross-domain conditions

Identify design judgments that depend on:

- firmware and startup timing;
- cooling and sustained load;
- acoustics, optics, or sensor mounting;
- enclosure, harness, and connector geometry;
- transient or fault behavior across multiple parts.

Continue directly when existing sources and ordinary calculations support the decision. When only hardware can answer it, convert it into a prototype test with a condition and observable result.

## Finish this stage

At the end of this stage, the manuscript should explain:

- what the product currently does;
- what it explicitly does not do;
- which functional blocks implement it;
- how power flows;
- how major interfaces connect;
- default and fault behavior;
- which open questions affect parts or connections;
- which questions are deferred to prototype testing.

Update the current handoff at the top, then use [1.2-parts.md](1.2-parts.md) to select the main parts and [2.1-schematic-contract.md](2.1-schematic-contract.md) to turn the design into exact pins and nets.
